# Tower RTS: plan v2 ("Expedition" pivot)

Written 2026-11-11 by Claude (Fable) after Jono's M3 feel test, for other models to carry out.
History and findings of M0–M3: `docs/plan-v1.md`. Rules as built: `DESIGN.md`. The pivot's design: `docs/expedition.md`. **Read all three, then `CLAUDE.md`, before coding.**

## Where we are
M0–M3 are live: tower building, economy, units, one lane, three AI levels, menu, overtime. 38 tests.
Jono's verdict: fun enough to continue, but head-to-head trooper streams are too thin. New direction:
**more Fallout Shelter, less StarCraft.** You mainly build up a tower. Now and then you get raided. You send units out
to explore to the left and right through fog, and find caches, raider camps, a friendly tower to trade with, and an enemy tower.
The current two-tower game stays in the menu as "Skirmish" and as a regression suite.

## Decisions for the pivot (defaults; Jono can overturn. Proceed on these until he does)
- E1 Map: a **1-D strip of sectors** left and right of your tower, not a 2-D grid. The game is a side view, so a strip needs
     no second map screen and "attacks from the left or right" falls out for free. (A 2-D overworld is a later option.)
- E2 Win: destroy the hidden enemy **Stronghold**. Lose: your Core falls. No overtime in Expedition. Target run: 10–15 min.
- E3 Units stay automatic. Control is one setting per production room, **Deploy: Home / Left / Right**, plus a tower-wide
     Recall. This is the hold/push lever (old Q4).
- E4 Keep Skirmish (M3) in the menu. Expedition becomes the default mode once P3 ships.
- E5 Friendly tower: a simple trade panel (minerals ↔ gas at a posted rate, plus one welcome gift). No diplomacy.
- E6 Tapping stays (old Q6). Production still spends freely (old Q7); revisit at the P3 feel test.

## P0: feedback fixes (small; do first)
- [x] Panel buttons flickered and swallowed clicks on desktop. Cause: `Hud.updatePanel` rebuilt `innerHTML` whenever any
      text changed (training % changes every tick at speed), so a button was replaced between mousedown and mouseup.
      Fixed: buttons rebuild only on structural change; text lines update separately (`setLines`).
- [x] Middle difficulty: level **Steady** (AI taps every 90 s ≈ 70% economy), between Relaxed (40%) and Sharp (100%).
      Any opponent can be played at any level, e.g. Expander/Steady.
- [x] **Permanent room panel** instead of a popup. Built as specified; in portrait the unit buttons sit on one row and drop
      their supply line so Collect/Demolish fit in 120 px. Spec:
      - Desktop (width ≥ 900): a fixed right-hand column, 280 px, full height under the top bar. Always visible.
      - Landscape phone (height ≤ 520): fixed left column, 210 px. The build palette stays on the right.
      - Portrait: a fixed strip above the palette, 120 px tall, content laid out in two columns.
      - Nothing selected → show a tower summary: income per second (minerals, gas, tapped vs untapped), supply used/max,
        units alive by type, and one hint line. A room selected → what the popup shows today. No close button; tapping
        empty space deselects.
      - `measureInsets()` in `src/main.ts` must subtract the panel so the camera frames the tower in the remaining area.
      - Keep the structural-key pattern from the flicker fix. Never rebuild buttons on a timer.
      - Verify with Playwright at the three sizes: tower fully visible, no overlap with palette, top bar or lane strip.
- Exit: Jono confirms the panel feels right on desktop and phone. ✅ (2026-11-11: "rad")

## P1: generalize the world (no new gameplay; Skirmish must still pass)
Goal: towers at arbitrary positions on one axis, attackable from both sides, more than two towers.
- [x] `Tower` gains `id`, `x` (left edge, world cells), `coreCol`, and `front: 'left' | 'right' | 'both'`.
      World x is in cells; a tower spans `[x, x + TOWER.cols)`; its faces are at `x` and `x + TOWER.cols`.
      Skirmish layout: home `x = 0` (front right), enemy `x = TOWER.cols + LANE.length` (front left, Core at cols 4–5).
      Units' `x` shifts by `+TOWER.cols` compared with today; `faceX(team)` is replaced by tower faces.
- [x] `World.towers` becomes a plain list. `w.tower(team)` returns that team's first tower (so most call sites survive).
      Commands gain an optional `tower` id (default: the team's first tower).
- [x] Teams: `0` player, `1` hostile, `2` neutral. Add `hostile(a, b)` (only 0↔1). Replace every "the other team" computation.
- [x] Units gain `dir: 1 | -1` and `home: towerId | null`. Supply counts only units whose `home` is that tower.
- [x] `frontRoom(tower, fromSide, reachesAllFloors)`: scan columns from the side the attacker stands on.
      A unit attacks the nearest hostile tower face ahead of it within reach; it stops at `face − dir × range`.
- [x] `pickCell` in the AI mirrors `front`/`back` by `tower.front`.
- [x] Renderer: draw every tower at `tower.x × CELL_W` with no mirroring; `laneToWorld(x) = x × CELL_W`. Camera presets
      cycle through known towers.
- [x] Tests: all 38 still pass after mechanical updates. Add: a tower hit from the left loses column 0 first; three towers
      on one axis; `hostile()` table; a neutral tower is never targeted.
- Exit: Skirmish plays exactly as before (`npm run match -- --all` numbers within noise of `docs/plan-v1.md`).
  ✅ Identical, line for line (e.g. rush vs boom 2-38 avg 3:53; boom mirror 22-18 avg 4:24). 44 tests.
  Known, unchanged from M3: a perfectly symmetric overtime tie goes to team 0 (`score(0) >= score(1)`), which is why
  the Rusher mirror reads 40-0. Skirmish-only; Expedition has no overtime.

## P2: sectors, fog, exploring, deploy orders
- [ ] `src/sim/map.ts`: sectors of `SECTOR = 14` cells, indices `−R..R` (`R = 5`), home tower centred in sector 0.
      `sectorOf(x)`. Seeded generation: each non-home sector gets one site from a weighted table (see `docs/expedition.md`),
      with guarantees: exactly one Stronghold at distance 3–5 on a random side, exactly one Trader at distance 2–4,
      at least one Cache within distance 2, no Camp at distance 1.
- [ ] Fog is sim state: `explored: Set<sector>`. A sector becomes explored when a player unit enters it. Sector 0 and ±1
      terrain start explored (but ±1 sites are still discovered on entry). `visible(sector)` = holds a player unit or tower,
      or is adjacent to one. Hostile units outside visible sectors are not drawn and not shown on the strip.
- [ ] Deploy orders: production rooms gain `deploy: 'home' | 'left' | 'right'` (command `setDeploy`, default `home`).
      Unit orders: `garrison` (hold at the home tower; engage any hostile in sector 0, then walk back) and `march(dir)`
      (advance, fight, attack hostile sites, stop at the map edge and hold). Commands `recall` (all → garrison) and
      `sendGarrison(dir)` (all garrison units → march).
- [ ] Sites as data. Cache: first player unit to reach it banks its loot (effect + toast). Ruin: nothing. Others in P3/P4.
- [ ] Client: sector strip replaces the lane strip (fog shown as hatching, discovered sites as icons, tap a sector to move
      the camera there); fog curtain drawn over unexplored sectors in the world view; Deploy buttons in the room panel;
      Recall / Send buttons in the tower summary.
- [ ] Menu: mode picker (Expedition / Skirmish). Expedition is marked "new" but not default yet.
- [ ] Tests: generation guarantees over 200 seeds; exploration reveals in order; garrison returns home; a cache pays once;
      determinism + replay with deploy commands.
- Exit: you can explore both directions, find caches, and see the fog lift.

## P3: raids and defense ← FEEL-TEST GATE
- [ ] `src/sim/systems/director.ts`: a deterministic raid director. First raid at 2:30, then every 60–90 s (seeded),
      alternating/weighted by side. Size grows with time (see table in `docs/expedition.md`). Raids spawn from the nearest
      living Camp on that side, else from the map edge. Emit a `warning` effect 12 s before; the client shows
      "Raid from the left in 12 s" and marks the side on the strip.
- [ ] Camps: small hostile towers from a preset layout (Core + 2–3 rooms, no production UI). Destroying one stops raids
      from it, grants loot, and lengthens the raid interval on that side.
- [ ] Defense rooms: **Gun Nest** (anti-ground) and **Flak** (anti-air). Must be on an outer column of their floor.
      They shoot hostiles within range of that face. Numbers in `docs/expedition.md`.
- [ ] Home tower for Expedition: Core in the middle (cols 2–3), `front: 'both'`.
- [ ] Balance tool: `npm run match -- --mode expedition --bot steady --n 100` runs a scripted player
      (an `AiController` on team 0 that also issues deploy orders) and prints: survival rate at 5/10/15 min, raids repelled,
      sectors explored. Target before the feel test: the bot survives to 10 min in 60–80% of seeds.
- [ ] Tests: raid schedule is deterministic per seed; warning precedes spawn; Gun Nest ignores air and Flak ignores ground;
      a destroyed Camp stops spawning.
- Exit: **Jono plays Expedition and answers the feel test at the bottom of this file.** Do not start P4 before that.

## P4: the other towers
- [ ] Stronghold: an AI-run hostile tower (reuse `AiController` + a script). Dormant (economy only) until discovered,
      then it sends its own units at you. Destroying its Core wins the run.
- [ ] Trader: a neutral tower. When a player unit reaches it, a Trade panel unlocks (works from home afterwards):
      minerals ↔ gas at a posted, slowly drifting rate, and a one-time gift. Hostiles ignore it.
- [ ] Run summary screen: time, sectors explored, raids repelled, rooms built. Best run saved locally (try/catch around storage).
- [ ] Make Expedition the default mode.
- Exit: a full run can be won; a scripted bot wins 30–60% of seeds at Steady.

## P5: Fallout Shelter depth (pick with Jono after P3; do not start unasked)
Candidates, roughly cheapest first: tech that pays (old M4 roster: Siege Walker, Interceptor, Lab upgrades) ·
"rush" a room for instant output at a risk · crew assigned to rooms with stats (old Q2) · room upgrades (levels) ·
incidents inside the tower (fire, breach) · a bigger tower that unlocks floors as you grow.

## Later
Mobile polish and PWA (old M5) · multiplayer lockstep · Jackbox mode · player-written automation (Auto-Starcraft tie-in) · AR.

## Feel test for P3 (Jono)
- [ ] Does "mostly building, sometimes raided, sometimes exploring" feel like the game you wanted?
- [ ] Raid frequency and size: too much, too little?
- [ ] Is Deploy (Home / Left / Right) enough control, too much, or too fiddly?
- [ ] Is exploring rewarding? What did you want to find out there?
- [ ] Which P5 candidate first?

